[38;5;231m⏺[39m Fourth question, then I'll put the approaches and design in front of you.

  The fork is [1mwhich vendor[0m, and it matters more than usual here because this repo has no build step: [38;5;153mindex.html[39m loads
  [38;5;153mapp.js[39m with a bare [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag, and [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no dependencies and no scripts. So the SDK arrives as a CDN
  [38;5;153m<script>[39m, and its config — the project key and the environment name — has to live somewhere a static page can read,
  which means it's public. That's normal and safe for all three (these keys are write-only ingest keys, not secrets),
  but it does mean anyone can post junk to your project, so the vendor's rate-limiting and spam controls are part of the
  choice.

  - [1mSentry (recommended).[0m Error-first, with breadcrumbs and input masking built in and a usable free tier. Buys the
    shortest path to exactly the scope you picked; costs the least setup. Roughly 30KB gzipped from CDN.
  - [1mDatadog RUM.[0m Strong if you already run Datadog and want browser errors next to backend traces. Buys correlation
    across your stack; costs a Datadog account and a heavier SDK, and it's overkill without existing Datadog usage.
  - [1mLogRocket.[0m Replay-first. You ruled out replay in the last answer, so most of what you'd pay for goes unused.

  I recommend [1mSentry[0m unless you're already a Datadog shop, in which case correlation with your existing dashboards wins.
